<header><h1>{title}</h1><p class="subtitle">A manifesto for a new national framework for rail mobility</p><p class="byline">By Miguel do Rosário · November 12, 2026</p></header>
<figure><img src={image} width="1024" height="682" alt="Rio de Janeiro's light rail vehicle on its inaugural journey through the city center" /><figcaption>Rio's light rail on its inaugural journey, June 5, 2016. Photo: Fernando Frazão/Agência Brasil · <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/br/">CC BY 3.0 Brazil</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<article>
<p>I have become that bore, I know, who can only talk about trains. But at least I am consistent, and I believe certain obsessions are healthy.</p>
<p>I have interviewed President Lula and President Dilma several times. Every time, I asked essentially the same question: why wasn't Brazil investing far more in railways?</p>
<p>Whenever I think about Brazil's development, the same image comes to mind. I see a Brazil crossed by high-speed railways, with major cities served by extensive metro and light rail networks.</p>
<p>That is why I am launching this manifesto. Lula's epic second-round victory has, in my view, opened a historic opportunity to expand public investment and put mobility at the heart of a new national project.</p>
<h3><strong>Industrialization and mobility</strong></h3>
<p>I have friends who are economists, independent professionals and Brazilians from all walks of life who advocate industrializing the country. My friend Elias Jabbour, for example, made this a campaign priority, as he emphasized in an interview with me.</p>
<p>I agree with them, but I believe we need to give that word concrete meaning. Industrializing Brazil requires tackling the cost and difficulty of moving people and goods.</p>
<p>I do not even want to confine this discussion to the term urban mobility. A national rail network can also connect the interior, serve rural regions and create opportunities in places that are now isolated.</p>
<p>We need an ambitious plan to connect our state capitals and major economic centers by passenger rail. High-speed rail must be part of that ambition, introduced in stages, with modern conventional and regional services completing the network.</p>
<p>In metropolitan regions, we need to build and expand metro systems. Where a metro is not the appropriate solution, we should invest in light rail and other integrated modes, according to each city's needs.</p>
<p>Where there are bays, rivers and navigable waterways, we need ferries. The goal is a comprehensive, accessible network designed to serve people.</p>
<h3><strong>Railways are for people, too</strong></h3>
<p>Brazil needs a major freight railway program. But we cannot treat the tracks as though their only purpose were to carry ore and grain to ports.</p>
<p>Railways are for people, too. Brazilians have the right to travel comfortably, safely and reliably, without depending exclusively on planes, buses or cars.</p>
<p>Our passenger rail network is very small relative to the country's territory, population and needs. Everyday life in Europe and China's railway expansion show possibilities we are still far from offering Brazilians.</p>
<p>Even the United States, so closely associated with the automobile, built a vast freight rail network. Railways were important to its industrial expansion, just as they were to British industrialization and the modernization of Japan and China.</p>
<p>Here, expanding the networks should generate orders for Brazilian industry. We can manufacture trains, freight cars, electrical equipment and signaling systems, developing technology and creating skilled jobs.</p>
<h3><strong>A national framework and a dedicated fund</strong></h3>
<p>I propose that Lula lead the creation of a new national framework for rail mobility. We need the institutional, financial and political architecture to sustain this transformation for decades.</p>
<p>The framework should coordinate federal, state and municipal governments, integrate networks and provide certainty for investment. It should establish targets, responsibilities, oversight and affordable fares, while also ensuring that services are maintained.</p>
<p>Its centerpiece would be a National Rail Mobility Fund. This would provide stable financing for the construction and expansion of passenger and freight networks.</p>
<p>Brazil can discuss a small levy on exports, whose producers would also benefit from better logistics. It can allocate part of public oil revenues to the fund, turning today's wealth into infrastructure for future generations.</p>
<p>The government should pursue international agreements and long-term financing from the BRICS bank, the World Bank and other development institutions. It can combine these resources with private investment and other domestic sources.</p>
<p>It is also possible to discuss the role of international reserves while preserving their function as protection against crises. This requires a risk assessment, just as loans require the ability to repay.</p>
<p>This is not about building everything without public resources, but about combining sources and spreading the effort over time. Mobility is as essential to development as water and electricity, and the limits of a single year's budget cannot stand in the way of a decades-long program.</p>
<h3><strong>An alliance for the transition</strong></h3>
<p>This transformation requires political judgment to bring in the sectors that currently transport Brazilians and their goods. Bus companies, freight carriers and airlines can become partners in investments and services, profiting from and contributing to the transition.</p>
<p>Buses can connect neighborhoods and smaller towns to stations, while trucks handle freight distribution at either end of the journey. Airlines can integrate their routes with railways and airports.</p>
<p>The government should build this alliance without abandoning public planning, competition or passengers' interests. Rail expansion can create opportunities for domestic partners rather than being presented as a war against them.</p>
<h3><strong>Cities need room to breathe</strong></h3>
<p>This proposal does not exclude investment in roads, which are indispensable in a country of continental dimensions. But our cities can no longer accommodate an endless increase in the number of cars.</p>
<p>We need to reduce dependence on cars by offering better alternatives, without blaming those who need them for work. Buses will remain essential, but when trapped in the same traffic jams as cars, they cannot meet the travel needs of millions of people on their own.</p>
<p>For a long time, buying a car represented a gain in freedom for working people. Today, for many, it means loan payments, fuel, taxes, maintenance and hours lost in traffic.</p>
<p>Owning a car cannot be a requirement for participating in city life. Fewer cars on the streets also make travel easier for those who need or prefer to drive.</p>
<h3><strong>Energy and the environment</strong></h3>
<p>Electric trains can take advantage of the share of renewable sources in our electricity mix. Planning energy, industry and transport together offers an opportunity to reduce dependence on fossil fuels.</p>
<p>We should expand residential solar power, reducing household costs and diversifying generation. This effort needs to go hand in hand with investment in the grid and in the capacity to supply industry and transport.</p>
<p>Railway projects must include rigorous environmental studies and protection for communities and ecosystems. Viaducts, tunnels and wildlife crossings can reduce impacts and preserve the movement of animals, depending on the characteristics of each project.</p>
<h3><strong>A cause for everyone</strong></h3>
<p>Rail mobility can help depolarize Brazil. It belongs neither to the left nor to the right, because it addresses a universal need.</p>
<p>It benefits Black and white people, men and women, LGBTQIA+ people, children, older people and people with and without disabilities. Everyone gains from transport that allows them to reach work, school, hospital or leisure activities with dignity.</p>
<p>This agenda can bring together workers, businesspeople and representatives of different parties. Its universal benefits do not prevent the progressive camp from taking the initiative to make it happen.</p>
<h3><strong>From 2028 to 2030</strong></h3>
<p>Mobility can be the central theme for progressive candidates in the 2028 municipal elections. Those elections will be a kind of midterm vote for Lula's government, contested in Brazilian cities.</p>
<p>With a national program underway, mayoral candidates will be able to present viable proposals for their municipalities and metropolitan regions. This can help win mayoralties and broaden support for the government in the second half of its term.</p>
<p>In this second round, it was clear to me that Lula represented a vision of the future for Brazil. But in 2030, Brazilians will want to see clearly what that future is.</p>
<p>The president needs to present it now, connecting mobility, industrialization, energy and the modernization of cities. Construction moving forward, jobs being created and services improving will give substance to that commitment.</p>
<p>A transformation on this scale could give Lula's successor a strong political foundation for a presidential bid. With visible results and major projects underway, it could even make a first-round victory possible.</p>
<p>Lula built his political career in the metalworking industry of the ABC region, when the automobile represented jobs and upward social mobility. The next cycle of development needs to broaden that industrial base and prioritize public transport.</p>
<p>It will not all be done in four years. But a new national framework, a dedicated fund and a broad alliance can begin a lasting national policy that outlives individual administrations.</p>
<p>If Brazil wants to bring about its own industrial revolution, it needs to build railways. Lula has the opportunity to begin that transformation now.</p>
<h3><strong>Mundo Trilhos: an invitation to collaborate</strong></h3>
<p>We have begun developing <a href="https://www.mundotrilhos.com/">Mundo Trilhos</a>, a map of railway systems in Brazil and around the world. We want to use this space to broaden the debate, bring interested people together in WhatsApp groups and forums, and organize seminars on the future of transport in the country.</p>
<p>This is a work in progress, open to collaboration. Anyone who would like to contribute data or ideas, or help organize these activities, can write to me at <a href="mailto:migueldorosario@gmail.com">migueldorosario@gmail.com</a>.</p>

</article>
